/**
 * @file FoundationTop.dox
 *
 * @page FoundationTopLevelHeaders Top-Level Headers
 * @ingroup Foundation
 *
 * @brief Public top-level include interface for Foundation.
 *
 * Foundation exposes a set of top-level headers intended to be used
 * directly by application code.
 *
 * These headers are especially important for Arduino, PSoC Creator,
 * and other embedded environments where users usually include headers
 * directly instead of relying on CMake targets.
 *
 * @section FoundationTop_Overview Overview
 *
 * The top-level headers define the public include surface of
 * Foundation.
 *
 * They provide a stable access layer that keeps application code
 * independent from the internal folder structure of the framework.
 *
 * User code should prefer:
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * #include <Foundation.h>
 * #include <Foundation_Math.h>
 * #include <Foundation_Time.h>
 * @endcode
 *
 * instead of including internal module headers directly:
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * #include <Foundation/Math/Ratio.h>
 * #include <Foundation/Time/Clock.h>
 * @endcode
 *
 * Internal headers may still exist and may be used inside the
 * Foundation source tree, but top-level headers are the recommended
 * public entry points for applications.
 *
 * @section FoundationTop_HeaderList Top-Level Header List
 *
 * Foundation provides the following top-level headers:
 *
 * @code
 * Foundation.h
 * Foundation_BuildSettings.h
 * Foundation_TypeTraits.h
 * Foundation_Math.h
 * Foundation_Time.h
 * Foundation_Containers.h
 * Foundation_Functional.h
 * Foundation_Scheduling.h
 * Foundation_Utils.h
 * @endcode
 *
 * @section FoundationTop_MainHeader Main Header
 *
 * @subsection FoundationTop_FoundationHeader Foundation.h
 *
 * The Foundation.h header is the main public entry point of the
 * framework.
 *
 * It includes the public interface of the enabled Foundation modules.
 *
 * Use this header when the application needs broad access to the
 * enabled Foundation functionality.
 *
 * Example:
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * #include <Foundation.h>
 *
 * void setup() {
 * }
 *
 * void loop() {
 * }
 * @endcode
 *
 * This include style is convenient for:
 *
 * - Arduino sketches.
 * - Examples.
 * - Prototypes.
 * - Small embedded applications.
 * - Applications that use several Foundation modules.
 *
 * @section FoundationTop_BuildSettingsHeader Build Settings Header
 *
 * @subsection FoundationTop_FoundationBuildSettingsHeader Foundation_BuildSettings.h
 *
 * The Foundation_BuildSettings.h header exposes build configuration
 * macros.
 *
 * These macros describe which modules or features are available in the
 * current build.
 *
 * Example:
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * #include <Foundation_BuildSettings.h>
 *
 * #ifdef FOUNDATION_MATH
 *     // Math module is available.
 * #endif
 * @endcode
 *
 * This header is useful when writing code that must adapt to different
 * Foundation distributions.
 *
 * @section FoundationTop_ModuleHeaders Module Headers
 *
 * Each Foundation module may expose a dedicated top-level header.
 *
 * These headers allow application code to include only the module-level
 * interface it needs.
 *
 * @subsection FoundationTop_TypeTraitsHeader Foundation_TypeTraits.h
 *
 * The Foundation_TypeTraits.h header exposes the Type Traits module.
 *
 * This module provides compile-time type utilities.
 *
 * Typical usage:
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * #include <Foundation_TypeTraits.h>
 * @endcode
 *
 * This header maps to:
 *
 * - @ref Foundation_TypeTraits
 *
 * @subsection FoundationTop_MathHeader Foundation_Math.h
 *
 * The Foundation_Math.h header exposes the Math module.
 *
 * Typical contents include:
 *
 * - Arithmetic helpers.
 * - Ratio utilities.
 * - Complex numbers.
 * - Fixed-size matrices.
 * - Dynamic matrices.
 * - Matrix operations.
 *
 * Typical usage:
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * #include <Foundation_Math.h>
 * @endcode
 *
 * This header maps to:
 *
 * - @ref Foundation_Math
 *
 * @subsection FoundationTop_TimeHeader Foundation_Time.h
 *
 * The Foundation_Time.h header exposes the Time module.
 *
 * Typical contents include:
 *
 * - Frequency.
 * - Period.
 * - Duration.
 * - Tick.
 * - TimePoint.
 * - Clock.
 *
 * Typical usage:
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * #include <Foundation_Time.h>
 * @endcode
 *
 * This header maps to:
 *
 * - @ref Foundation_Time
 *
 * @subsection FoundationTop_ContainersHeader Foundation_Containers.h
 *
 * The Foundation_Containers.h header exposes the Containers module.
 *
 * This module is intended for reusable storage abstractions such as
 * arrays, buffers, queues, lists, or other container types.
 *
 * Typical usage:
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * #include <Foundation_Containers.h>
 * @endcode
 *
 * This header maps to:
 *
 * - @ref Foundation_Containers
 *
 * @subsection FoundationTop_FunctionalHeader Foundation_Functional.h
 *
 * The Foundation_Functional.h header exposes the Functional module.
 *
 * This module is intended for callable utilities, callback helpers,
 * delegates, and related abstractions.
 *
 * Typical usage:
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * #include <Foundation_Functional.h>
 * @endcode
 *
 * This header maps to:
 *
 * - @ref Foundation_Functional
 *
 * @subsection FoundationTop_SchedulingHeader Foundation_Scheduling.h
 *
 * The Foundation_Scheduling.h header exposes the Scheduling module.
 *
 * This module is intended for task scheduling, periodic execution,
 * one-shot execution, and embedded-friendly execution control.
 *
 * Typical usage:
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * #include <Foundation_Scheduling.h>
 * @endcode
 *
 * This header maps to:
 *
 * - @ref Foundation_Scheduling
 *
 * @subsection FoundationTop_UtilsHeader Foundation_Utils.h
 *
 * The Foundation_Utils.h header exposes the Utils module.
 *
 * This module contains general-purpose utilities that do not belong to
 * a more specific module.
 *
 * Typical usage:
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * #include <Foundation_Utils.h>
 * @endcode
 *
 * This header maps to:
 *
 * - @ref Foundation_Utils
 *
 * @section FoundationTop_IncludeStrategies Include Strategies
 *
 * Foundation supports two main include strategies:
 *
 * - Full include.
 * - Selective include.
 *
 * @subsection FoundationTop_FullInclude Full Include
 *
 * Use the main Foundation.h header when the application needs access
 * to several modules or when simplicity is preferred.
 *
 * Example:
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * #include <Foundation.h>
 * @endcode
 *
 * This style is recommended for:
 *
 * - Arduino sketches.
 * - PSoC Creator applications.
 * - Examples.
 * - Quick tests.
 * - Small applications.
 *
 * @subsection FoundationTop_SelectiveInclude Selective Include
 *
 * Use individual module headers when the application only needs
 * specific parts of the framework.
 *
 * Example:
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * #include <Foundation_Math.h>
 * #include <Foundation_Time.h>
 * @endcode
 *
 * This style is recommended for:
 *
 * - Library code.
 * - Public headers.
 * - Larger projects.
 * - Embedded applications with strict dependency control.
 * - Projects where compile-time dependencies should remain explicit.
 *
 * @section FoundationTop_ArduinoUsage Arduino Usage
 *
 * Arduino users should normally include Foundation through top-level
 * headers.
 *
 * Basic example:
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * #include <Foundation.h>
 *
 * void setup() {
 * }
 *
 * void loop() {
 * }
 * @endcode
 *
 * Selective include example:
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * #include <Foundation_Math.h>
 * #include <Foundation_Time.h>
 *
 * void setup() {
 * }
 *
 * void loop() {
 * }
 * @endcode
 *
 * Top-level headers avoid exposing Arduino sketches to the internal
 * source tree layout of Foundation.
 *
 * @section FoundationTop_PSoCUsage PSoC Creator Usage
 *
 * In PSoC Creator projects, Foundation is usually consumed through an
 * exported distribution containing:
 *
 * - Public headers.
 * - Precompiled static libraries.
 * - Build settings.
 *
 * Application code should include top-level headers from the exported
 * include directory.
 *
 * Example:
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * #include <Foundation.h>
 * @endcode
 *
 * or:
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * #include <Foundation_Math.h>
 * @endcode
 *
 * @section FoundationTop_DesktopUsage Desktop Usage
 *
 * Desktop projects may use the same top-level headers as embedded
 * projects.
 *
 * When using CMake, the recommended workflow is:
 *
 * - Add or find the Foundation package.
 * - Link the Foundation target.
 * - Include the required top-level headers.
 *
 * Example:
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * #include <Foundation.h>
 * @endcode
 *
 * @section FoundationTop_PublicApiPolicy Public API Policy
 *
 * The top-level headers define the intended public include interface of
 * Foundation.
 *
 * Application code should treat these headers as the stable entry
 * points of the framework.
 *
 * Headers located deeper inside the Foundation source tree may be
 * reorganized as the framework evolves.
 *
 * Recommended:
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * #include <Foundation_Math.h>
 * @endcode
 *
 * Avoid in application code:
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * #include <Foundation/Math/Matrix/FixedMatrix/FixedMatrix.h>
 * @endcode
 *
 * Internal headers may still be documented, but they should not
 * necessarily be considered the preferred user-facing include path.
 *
 * @section FoundationTop_HeaderResponsibility Header Responsibility
 *
 * Top-level headers should:
 *
 * - Expose the public module interface.
 * - Include only public module headers.
 * - Avoid exposing private implementation details.
 * - Remain stable across internal folder reorganizations.
 * - Match the enabled build configuration.
 *
 * Top-level headers should not:
 *
 * - Contain large implementations unless required by templates.
 * - Include unrelated modules unnecessarily.
 * - Become a replacement for clear module boundaries.
 * - Expose experimental internals as public API.
 *
 * @section FoundationTop_ModuleAvailability Module Availability
 *
 * The exact contents of each top-level header may depend on the
 * enabled modules in the current build.
 *
 * Reduced embedded builds may expose fewer modules than full desktop
 * builds.
 *
 * Code that depends on optional modules should check the corresponding
 * build macros when necessary.
 *
 * Example:
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * #include <Foundation_BuildSettings.h>
 *
 * #ifdef FOUNDATION_TIME
 *     #include <Foundation_Time.h>
 * #endif
 * @endcode
 *
 * @section FoundationTop_RecommendedUsage Recommended Usage
 *
 * For most applications:
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * #include <Foundation.h>
 * @endcode
 *
 * For module-specific application code:
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * #include <Foundation_Math.h>
 * @endcode
 *
 * For library headers:
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * #include <Foundation_Math.h>
 * #include <Foundation_Time.h>
 * @endcode
 *
 * For conditional builds:
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * #include <Foundation_BuildSettings.h>
 *
 * #ifdef FOUNDATION_MATH
 *     #include <Foundation_Math.h>
 * #endif
 * @endcode
 *
 * @section FoundationTop_RelatedTopics Related Topics
 *
 * Related Foundation documentation:
 *
 * - @ref Foundation
 * - @ref Foundation_TypeTraits
 * - @ref Foundation_Math
 * - @ref Foundation_Time
 * - @ref Foundation_Containers
 * - @ref Foundation_Functional
 * - @ref Foundation_Scheduling
 * - @ref Foundation_Utils
 */